You are reproducing a Python command-line utility from scratch.
Write a single, self-contained Python 3 script (intended for Termux on Android,
with the shebang `#!/data/data/com.termux/files/usr/bin/env python`) that
benchmarks and compares many compression formats and codecs against a given
input file or directory. **Purpose:** The script takes a source file or
directory, packages it as a tar archive if it is a directory, then compresses
that data with a large set of compressors, timing each run and reporting the
output size, compression ratio, and elapsed time.
Results should be printed in a readable, sorted or tabular form so the user can
compare formats. **Inputs:** - A path to a file or directory supplied via
command-line arguments (`sys.argv`).
Use `sys.exit` with a usage message if arguments are missing or the path does
not exist. - Optional compression level(s) per codec, where applicable, with
sensible defaults per format. **Outputs:** - Compressed artifacts written into a
temporary or designated output directory, named by format and level. - A console
report listing, for each compressor: format name, level, compressed size
(human-readable via a helper that formats bytes into B/KB/MB/GB/TB/PB with two
decimals), compression ratio versus original size, and wall-clock time.
Clean up temporary files at the end unless configured otherwise. **Required
structure and behavior:** - Use `from __future__ import annotations`, and
standard-library modules only for core functionality: `bz2`, `gzip`, `lzma`,
`shutil`, `sys`, `tarfile`, `tempfile`, `time`, `zipfile`, and `pathlib.Path`,
with `typing.Callable` and `Optional`. - Define a type alias for compressor
functions with the signature: takes a source Path, a destination Path, and an
optional integer level, and returns nothing. - Define a `human(n)` helper that
loops through units up to TB and falls back to PB. - Define a `make_tar(src_dir,
tar_path)` helper that creates an uncompressed tar archive with the directory's
name as the arcname. - Define a `_read_all(src)` helper that reads a whole file
as bytes for in-memory codecs. - Optional third-party codecs must be imported
inside `try/except ImportError` blocks, setting the module to `None` on failure,
so the script still runs with only the standard library.
Cover these optional libraries: brotli, zstandard, lz4.frame, py7zr, pylzma,
cramjam, zopfli.gzip, pyppmd, pyzstd, zipfile_deflate64, and pyzipper. - Build a
registry of compressors (name → function) covering the standard-library formats
(gzip, bz2, lzma/xz, zip with deflate, tar variants) and each optional codec
only when its module is available; skip unavailable ones silently or with a
note. - Each compressor function must handle its own level argument, falling
back to a default when none is given, and must write to the destination path. -
Gracefully handle per-codec exceptions: catch errors for an individual format,
report it as failed or skipped in the output, and continue with the remaining
formats rather than aborting the whole run. - Handle edge cases: empty files,
missing paths, directories versus single files, unreadable files, zero-byte
compressed output (avoid division by zero in ratio calculation), and filenames
with special characters. - Use `time.perf_counter()` for timing each compression
run. - Use `tempfile` for scratch output so results are isolated, and remove
temporary artifacts in a `finally` path. - Guard the entry point with `if
__name__ == "__main__":` and exit with a nonzero status on fatal usage errors. -
Keep the code clear and idiomatic: type hints on function signatures, docstrings
or brief comments where logic is non-obvious, and no external dependencies
beyond the optional codecs listed above. **Execution:** Run as `python script.py
<path> [options]` in Termux.
The script should work with only the standard library installed and
automatically enable extra formats when the optional packages are present. ---
